Reverse Cutoff$^\ddagger$ & 2 & 0.839 $\pm$ 0.072 & 0.718 $\pm$ 0.025 & --- & $-$6.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). Within the fully v2 BN cells, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$4.2\,pp (Full OCC 0.725 vs.\ Detach OCC 0.767; Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.108, 0.014]$, crossing zero and thus not individually significant at $\alpha{=}0.05$ given the small sample). The LN cells show the same directional pattern ($-$2.5\,pp), yielding a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type, with the BN cell showing a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though Full OCC+LN has $n{=}1$ (v2) and Detach+LN has $n{=}3$ (v1, marked $^\star$). We report effect sizes and confidence intervals for transparency; the small-$n$ factorial design prioritizes breadth across a 12-condition space over statistical power for individual pairwise tests. The consistent gradient flow effect---both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF---provides the primary evidence for the ordinal interaction pattern.

\textbf{Ordinal, not additive.} Both BN and LN lines slope downward from Gradient OFF to Gradient ON without crossing (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}), confirming an ordinal pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though Full OCC+LN has only $n{=}1$ (v2) and Detach+LN has $n{=}3$ (v1, marked $^\star$); whether normalization type modulates the gradient flow effect requires additional seeds to confirm.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial: normalization type $\times$ gradient flow. Both lines slope downward (consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern): gradient flow is associated with lower CAR. The BN cell shows a larger deficit in current data (Full OCC+LN: $n{=}1$, v2; Detach+LN: $n{=}3$, v1$^\star$).}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF & $\Delta$(ON$-$OFF) \\
\midrule
BatchNorm    & Full OCC (0.725) & Detach OCC (\textbf{0.767}) & $-$4.2 \\
LayerNorm    & Full OCC+LN (0.740) & Detach+LN$^\star$ (0.765) & $-$2.5 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$(BN$-$LN) & $-$1.5 & $+$0.2 & interaction: 1.7 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/factorial_2x2.pdf}
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial interaction: normalization type (BN/LN) $\times$ gradient flow (ON/OFF). Both lines slope downward: gradient flow degrades CAR under BN ($-$4.2\,pp) and LN ($-$2.5\,pp), consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit; Full OCC+LN has $n{=}1$ (v2); Detach+LN has $n{=}3$ (v1, marked $^\star$). Error bars: cross-seed SD.}
\label{fig:factorial}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}, Appendix) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC, with conflict rates of ${\sim}$50\%. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, ruling out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content does not affect outcomes.

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed interaction pattern cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape. CKA analysis~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), localizing the safety degradation to optimization dynamics rather than representational corruption.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

